ITEMS=[]
def family(options,rows):
 for i,(q,e) in enumerate(rows):ITEMS.append((q,[options[i]]+options[:i]+options[i+1:],e))
family(['Conditions of sale','Financing terms','Market conditions','Property rights conveyed'],[
('A nominally comparable sale was between relatives at a concessionary price unrelated to market exposure. Which element of comparison first requires investigation?','The relationship and motivation of the parties concern conditions of sale; the price cannot simply be assumed representative.'),
('A seller accepted a long-term note at an interest rate well below the prevailing market rate. Which element requires cash-equivalent analysis?','Nonmarket credit arrangements concern financing terms and can influence the recorded price.'),
('A comparable sold during a demonstrably weaker demand period, before a market-wide price rise. Which element addresses this difference?','The adjustment concerns changed market conditions, not the passage of time alone.'),
('A comparable transferred a landlord\'s interest subject to a long below-market lease, while the subject assignment values an unleased fee simple interest. Which element differs?','Different ownership interests and lease encumbrances concern the property rights conveyed.')])
family(['Site size','Dwelling condition','Living area','Location'],[
('Two otherwise comparable houses stand on 5,000- and 9,000-square-foot lots with different supported yard utility. Which property element requires analysis?','The described difference is site size; a market-supported contribution must be determined rather than assumed proportional to area.'),
('The subject has maintained finishes, while a comparable needs extensive interior repairs. Their size and position are alike. Which element differs?','The repair and maintenance difference concerns condition, not necessarily construction quality or age.'),
('Both dwellings have the same room count and condition, but one contains 300 additional square feet of finished residential space under the same measurement convention. Which element differs?','The difference is living area; equal room counts do not establish equal size.'),
('Similar houses are separated by a boundary between two distinct competing submarkets with verified price differences. Which element requires analysis?','The differing market setting concerns location; distance alone does not measure its contribution.')])
family(['Verify the transaction','Convert financing to cash equivalence','Apply a market-conditions adjustment','Remove personal-property value'],[
('A database labels a transfer as a sale, but neither the price nor whether it was arm\'s length has been confirmed. Which task addresses this information gap?','Verification establishes the circumstances and reliability of the transaction before relying on its price.'),
('A verified sale used a nonmarket seller loan. A reliable present-value analysis of that note is available. Which task directly places its consideration on a cash basis?','The loan terms must be translated to their cash-equivalent effect; changing the appraisal date does not solve a financing difference.'),
('All other elements have been addressed, but supported comparable-market evidence shows a price change between contract date and the effective date. Which task remains?','Apply the supported market-conditions adjustment to reflect the change between relevant dates.'),
('The verified purchase price includes furniture with a separately supported contributory amount. The assignment concerns real property alone. Which task separates the relevant consideration?','Remove the supported non-real-property component to isolate the real property price.')])
family(['Paired-sales analysis','Regression analysis','Cost analysis','Income capitalization'],[
('Several closely matched sale pairs differ mainly in garage capacity. The appraiser isolates that difference to estimate an adjustment. Which technique is used?','Paired analysis seeks the price effect of a difference while controlling other material influences.'),
('A model estimates price relationships using many sales and several explanatory variables simultaneously. Which adjustment technique is described?','Regression estimates statistical relationships among dependent and explanatory variables; its data and assumptions still require appraisal judgment.'),
('An appraiser studies current construction expense and depreciation to estimate the contribution of a small outbuilding, checking the result against market behavior. Which technique supports the adjustment?','Cost analysis can inform a feature adjustment, but cost must be related to its market contribution.'),
('A parking feature yields a supported additional annual net income, which is converted to a value increment using a market rate. Which technique is used?','Capitalizing the income difference estimates a value contribution from its income-producing benefit.')])
family(['Comparable Alder','Comparable Birch','Comparable Cedar','Comparable Dogwood'],[
('Four sales are equally similar physically and need equal total adjustments. Alder has verified buyer and seller interviews; Birch has an unconfirmed price; Cedar has an unexplained related-party transfer; Dogwood has an unresolved package allocation. Which has the strongest stated verification support?','Alder\'s transaction evidence is verified. The others have specific unresolved reliability issues, despite similar physical comparability.'),
('The subject is a leased fee interest with market rent. Alder is a vacant fee simple sale; Birch has an equivalent lease at market rent; Cedar transfers only a tenant interest; Dogwood transfers a life estate. Other attributes are equal. Which directly matches the interest being valued?','Birch matches the subject\'s leased fee interest and rent circumstances. Different rights would require separate analysis.'),
('Market conditions changed abruptly last month. All sales are equally similar and verified: Alder contracted eight months ago, Birch six months ago, Cedar last week and Dogwood a year ago. Which avoids reliance on an uncertain adjustment across the market break?','Cedar contracted after the shift and most closely reflects current conditions, assuming the stated similarities.'),
('Four verified contemporaneous houses otherwise match the subject. Alder needs a major location adjustment, Birch a major size adjustment, Cedar a major condition adjustment, and Dogwood only a minor garage adjustment. Which has the strongest stated comparability?','Dogwood requires the smallest material change on the stated facts; the selection is based on the reliability of comparison, not simply its price.')])
family(['$360,000','$370,000','$380,000','$390,000'],[
('A comparable sold for $350,000 and is inferior to the subject by a supported $10,000 in garage utility. No other adjustments apply. What is its adjusted indication?','Add the contribution of the subject\'s superior utility to the comparable: $350,000 + $10,000 = $360,000.'),
('A comparable sold for $385,000. Its superior view warrants a $25,000 downward adjustment, while its inferior condition warrants $10,000 upward. What is the adjusted indication?','The net adjustment is -$25,000 + $10,000 = -$15,000; $385,000 - $15,000 = $370,000.'),
('A verified $400,000 package price includes personal property contributing $20,000. With no other adjustments, what real-property indication remains?','Subtract the separately supported personal-property contribution: $400,000 - $20,000 = $380,000.'),
('A comparable\'s cash-equivalent price is $375,000. Supported market change requires an upward adjustment of 4% of that amount, with no other differences. What is the adjusted indication?','$375,000 × 1.04 = $390,000; the adjustment is $15,000.')])
family(['$140 per square foot','$150 per square foot','$160 per square foot','$170 per square foot'],[
('A $280,000 comparable has 2,000 square feet of living area. What is its unadjusted sale price per square foot?','Divide the total price by the area: $280,000 / 2,000 = $140 per square foot.'),
('A $300,000 house sale includes furniture contributing $15,000. It has 1,900 square feet of living area. What is the real-property price per square foot after removing furniture?','The real-property price is $285,000; $285,000 / 1,900 = $150 per square foot.'),
('A comparable\'s price is $152 per square foot before a supported $8-per-square-foot upward location adjustment. What unit indication follows?','Apply the unit adjustment to the unit price: $152 + $8 = $160 per square foot.'),
('A 1,500-square-foot comparable yields a $255,000 adjusted indication after all required changes. What is the adjusted price per square foot?','$255,000 / 1,500 = $170 per square foot. Do not reapply adjustments already reflected in the total.')])
family(['$6,000','$9,000','$12,000','$15,000'],[
('Two verified sales are alike except for an extra bath: one sells for $266,000 and the other for $260,000. What preliminary bath contribution does this isolated pair indicate?','The isolated difference is $266,000 - $260,000 = $6,000; additional evidence would help confirm it.'),
('A house with a deck sold for $284,000 and one without for $270,000. The first also has a separately supported $5,000 superior view. After accounting for the view, what deck contribution is indicated?','The total difference is $14,000; subtract the $5,000 view contribution to isolate $9,000 for the deck.'),
('A verified garage adjustment is $60 per additional square foot of garage area, not living area. The subject has 200 more garage square feet than the comparable. What is the adjustment magnitude?','The supported marginal contribution is 200 × $60 = $12,000; the whole-house unit price is not the adjustment rate.'),
('Two otherwise identical rental properties differ by $900 in annual net income because of a feature. At a supported 6% capitalization rate, what feature contribution is indicated?','Capitalize the income increment: $900 / 0.06 = $15,000.')])
family(['2%','4%','6%','8%'],[
('A comparable sold for $250,000. Total signed adjustments add $5,000. What is the net adjustment percentage of the sale price?','$5,000 / $250,000 = 0.02, or 2%.'),
('Adjustments to a $300,000 sale are +$8,000 and -$4,000. What is the gross adjustment percentage, using absolute adjustments?','Gross adjustments total $12,000, even though net adjustments are $4,000; $12,000 / $300,000 = 4%.'),
('A stable property resold from $200,000 to $212,000 with no material changes or unusual sale conditions. What total price growth does this pair indicate?','The increase is $12,000; $12,000 / $200,000 = 6% for the stated interval.'),
('A comparable\'s original price is $250,000 and its adjusted indication is $230,000. What is the magnitude of the net downward adjustment as a percentage of the original price?','The decrease is $20,000; $20,000 / $250,000 = 8%.')])
family(['An upward adjustment','A downward adjustment','No adjustment for that feature','Additional evidence before adjustment'],[
('The subject has a two-car garage; the comparable has one. Market evidence supports a positive contribution for the additional garage capacity. What direction applies to the comparable\'s price?','Increase the inferior comparable\'s price to reflect the subject\'s supported superior utility.'),
('The comparable has a valuable lake view and the subject lacks it. The contribution is reliably supported. What direction applies to the comparable\'s price?','Reduce the superior comparable\'s price to reflect the subject\'s lesser view utility.'),
('The subject and comparable have different paint colors, but credible market evidence indicates no price effect from this difference. What treatment is supported?','A physical difference does not require a nonzero adjustment when it has no supported effect on value.'),
('A listing agent suggests that a solar system adds value, but its ownership, lease obligations and market contribution are all unknown. What is needed before selecting an amount or sign?','Investigate the legal and economic facts and market response; the label solar does not establish a reliable adjustment by itself.')])
family(['$305,000','$315,000','$325,000','$335,000'],[
('A sale includes $100,000 cash and a seller note with a supported cash-equivalent value of $205,000. What cash-equivalent consideration does the seller receive?','Add cash and the note\'s present cash-equivalent value: $100,000 + $205,000 = $305,000.'),
('A $330,000 sale price includes a concession shown by market analysis to increase the price by $15,000. What price results after removing that supported effect?','Subtract the measured price effect, not an assumed blanket concession percentage: $330,000 - $15,000 = $315,000.'),
('A comparable\'s $300,000 cash-equivalent price receives a $10,000 market-time increase and $15,000 for an inferior feature. What adjusted indication follows?','The stated dollar adjustments are additive: $300,000 + $10,000 + $15,000 = $325,000.'),
('A verified comparable indicates $345,000 after time and property adjustments. A separately identified non-real-property component of $10,000 has not yet been removed. What indication remains?','Remove the outstanding component once: $345,000 - $10,000 = $335,000.')])
family(['Bracketing the subject','Avoiding double counting','Testing a model outside its data range','Distinguishing correlation from cause'],[
('An appraiser chooses one smaller and one larger sale, both otherwise similar, to help place the subject within an observed size range. What analysis choice is illustrated?','Bracketing places the subject\'s characteristic between comparable observations rather than relying entirely on extrapolation.'),
('A model\'s condition adjustment already includes the effect of a worn roof. The appraiser checks whether a separate roof deduction would repeat that effect. What issue is being addressed?','The same value influence should not be deducted twice under different adjustment labels.'),
('A model fitted only to 900–1,600-square-foot houses is proposed for a 4,500-square-foot house. What specific concern should be investigated?','The subject lies far outside the observed data range, so the model would be extrapolating without direct support from similar observations.'),
('Sales with large garages also have better views. An analyst asks whether the apparent garage premium really reflects the view difference. What issue is being investigated?','An observed association does not isolate cause when another value-influencing variable differs systematically.')])
family(['$402,000','$408,000','$414,000','$420,000'],[
('An analyst has justified 80% weight on a $400,000 adjusted sale and 20% on a $410,000 sale. What weighted indication follows from those stated weights?','0.80 × $400,000 + 0.20 × $410,000 = $402,000. The weights require justification; they are supplied here.'),
('A comparable sold for $425,000. Supported adjustments are -$12,000 for location, +$5,000 for condition and -$10,000 for excess site utility. What is the adjusted indication?','$425,000 - $12,000 + $5,000 - $10,000 = $408,000.'),
('A comparable\'s cash-equivalent price is $400,000. Apply a supported 2% market increase first, then a $6,000 upward physical adjustment. What is the indication?','$400,000 × 1.02 + $6,000 = $414,000; the physical adjustment is not part of the percentage base.'),
('A similar sale supports $410,000 before adjustments. The subject\'s extra amenity adds $18,000, but its inferior location reduces value by $8,000. What indication results?','$410,000 + $18,000 - $8,000 = $420,000.')])
assert len(ITEMS)==52
